<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  The app is UNSANDBOXED on purpose, which is why com.apple.security.app-sandbox
  is absent and why the file-access keys that only mean something inside the
  sandbox went with it.

  Three things this app does that the sandbox forbids:
    - it spawns and supervises a llama-server child. The sandbox allows
      process-exec only inside the app bundle and a short list of system
      directories, and a child always inherits the parent's sandbox.
    - it reads a models folder the user picks anywhere on disk, from that
      CHILD process, which no security-scoped bookmark can grant.
    - it stores tens of gigabytes of weights outside a container the user
      cannot find or move.
  Every comparable local-model app ships the same way. Notarization does not
  require the sandbox.

  What replaces it: the hardened runtime, plus Gatekeeper's notarization
  check. The hardened runtime is neither an entitlement nor an Xcode build
  setting here — it is a codesign flag (--options runtime) that dist/sign.sh
  applies in its Developer ID branch and nowhere else, because an ad-hoc
  build carrying it cannot load its own ad-hoc dylibs. Distribution signing
  happens in dist/sign.sh, not in Xcode.

  The two network keys stay because they cost nothing and document intent:
  the app talks to Microsoft Graph and to a loopback model server.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>com.apple.security.network.client</key>
	<true/>
	<key>com.apple.security.network.server</key>
	<true/>
</dict>
</plist>
